1. Introduction
Food systems have been under unprecedented challenges over the last few years due to climate change, human population growth, increased urbanization, and the outbreak of pandemics, as well as violence and conflicts in many parts of the world. Therefore, global food security has become a pressing issue and received much attention from scientific communities and academic institutions (Mehrabi et al., 2022; Tamasiga et al., 2023). Among the significant dilemmas that face implementing food security strategies and principles of food sustainability is food waste and loss, since a huge amount of food is lost or wasted along the food supply chain. Indeed, according to many references, one-third of all food produced is wasted globally, which corresponds to 1.3 billion tons of edible food each year (Mokrane et al., 2023; Roy et al., 2023; Stefanini and Vignali, 2023). Consequently, reducing and better valorizing food waste and by-products have been widely recommended as efficient strategies to maximize the benefits of available resources and improve food security, sustainability, and circularity in food systems (Aschemann-witzel et al., 2023; Rakesh and Mahendran, 2024; Thorsen et al., 2024). Plant-derived waste and by-products, such as peel, leaves, seeds, bran, kernel, pomace, and oil cake can be obtained from a wide variety of fruit, vegetables, cereals, oil crops, root and tubers and other legumes during the primary production and the subsequent processing and distribution stages (Difonzo et al., 2021; Q. Jin et al., 2018; Pathania and Kaur, 2022). Growing research shows that such waste and by-products can be valorized by the extraction of valuable nutrients and compounds, such as proteins, vitamins, fatty acids, amino acids, dietary fibers, flavonoids, and phenolic compounds, among others (Ali et al., 2021; Hadidi et al., 2024; Joshi et al., 2022; Nirmal et al., 2023). The extracted components and bioactive compounds have been reported to have several health benefitting attributes to various health complications such as diabetes, cancer, and cardiovascular and neurocognitive diseases, mainly due to their antioxidant and antimicrobial activities (Perra et al., 2022; Szabo et al., 2022). Although thermal treatments and other conventional methods have been widely used to recover high-added-value compounds from food waste and by-products, recent years have witnessed an accelerated development of green technologies and other emerging approaches. Unlike traditional methods, emerging techniques such as microwave, ultrasound, supercritical fluid, and pulsed electric field enable reduced processing time, improved yield, and better quality of the extracted compounds (Calleja-G ́omez et al., 2024; Esparza et al., 2020; Venugopal et al., 2023). Since the emergence of the fourth industrial revolution (called Industry 4.0), a growing emphasis has been placed on the convergence of physical, digital, and biological technological innovations (Hassoun et al., 2022b; Hassoun et al., 2023a, 2023b, 2023c, 2023d, 2024). The invention of steam engine and the emergence of the first factories during the first industrial revolution were followed by the arrival of electricity and the introduction of the concept of mass production during the second industrial revolution. The third industrial revolution began in the 1970s, through partial automation and the advent of the first computers and electronic devices, while the ongoing Industry 4.0 has been
characterized by the rapid development of advanced technological innovations such as artificial intelligence, big data, smart sensors, 3D printing, digital twins, robotics, blockchain, among others (Hassoun, 2024; Liebig et al., 2023). Due to their high potential and beneficial characteristics, the application of these technologies has increased in many agriculture and food related sectors. From Fig. 1, it can be noticed that the number of publications and citations on the use of Industry 4.0 in food waste and loss has increased significantly over the last few years. Recent publications have shown that Industry 4.0 technologies offer significant opportunities in reducing and valorizing waste and by-products in the seafood (Hassoun et al., 2023c) and the dairy (Hassoun et al., 2024) sectors. In another recent publication, it was shown that harnessing Industry 4.0 technologies, especially artificial intelligence, big data, blockchain, smart sensors, 3D printing, as well as recent advances in nanobiotechnology could help reduce food waste, creating new food packaging solutions (Hassoun et al., 2023a, 2023b, 2023c, 2023d). Additionally, it was demonstrated in a recent study that Industry 4.0 could enable unlock the circularity of resources within food supply chains, leading to reduced food loss and waste (Lopes de Sousa Jabbour et al., 2021). While our previous work and review publications explored the role of Industry 4.0 technologies in achieving sustainable development goals across the broader food sector, this review specifically focuses on the transformation of plant-based waste and by-products into valuable products. The work highlights the application of Food Industry 4.0 enabling technologies in waste valorization, offering a targeted analysis of innovative solutions for addressing waste management challenges within the plant-based food sector. This review presents novel insights and detailed discussions on specific technologies and methodologies that were not covered in the previous work, thereby contributing new knowledge to the field.
2. Overview of plant-based waste and by-products
Plant-based food production generates various by-products with valuable potential (Fig. 2). Fruit and vegetable processing discards peels, stems, leaves, and seeds, which are often rich in fiber, alkaloids and vitamins (Nabi et al., 2024). Similarly, grain and cereal processing yields nutrient-rich byproducts like bran and germ, containing protein, fiber, and micronutrients (F ̆arcaș et al., 2022). Oilseed processing, after extracting the main oil, generates meal or cake rich in protein, polyphenols, and residual oil (Sa ́ et al., 2021). These readily accessible, inherently biodegradable plant by-products contribute to sustainable practices. Leveraging them through the recovery of valuable bioactive compounds presents a significant opportunity to contribute to the United Nations' 2030 Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).
2.1. Fruits and vegetables by-products
Fruits and vegetables generate substantial quantities of waste, comprising stems, leaves, peels, and seeds (Tahir et al., 2023). Due to their inherent attributes, high quantities of waste and by-products from fruits and vegetables can be generated during the selection, washing,
