cleaning, peeling, and nucleating procedures involved in their industrial processing. Furthermore, the presence of enzymes makes them susceptible to enzymatic browning, thus making waste generation an unavoidable outcome during the processing stage (Zhu et al., 2023). In developing countries, fruit and vegetable waste ranges from 15 % to 50 % of harvested produce and is typically generated before reaching the market (Gardas et al., 2017). Worldwide, approximately 0.3 and 15.6 million tons of kiwi residue and citrus peel waste are generated annually, respectively (Zhu et al., 2023). Furthermore, global grape pomace from grape processing accounts for 5-9 million tons annually (Zhu et al., 2023). By-products from fruits and vegetables are rich in various beneficial compounds, including vitamins, carotenoids, alkaloids, proteins, dietary fibers, and polyphenols. Fruit waste, in particular, comprises cellulose, lignin, hemicellulose, and other polysaccharides (Nabi et al., 2024). Therefore, there is considerable potential to recover these natural components from such residues within the framework of the circular economy. The citrus industry generates around 50–60 % of by-products, totaling over 60 million tons globally (Wedamulla et al., 2022). These residual materials comprise peels, pulp, seeds, and leaves and are rich in bioactive compounds like polyphenols, carotenoids, pectin, essential oils, and enzymes. Orange peels are among the most prevalent waste products in the food industry and serve as a rich source of carotenoids. The carotenoid content in orange peels is estimated to be around 97.4 μg/g when acetone is employed as a solvent (Murador et al., 2019). An enhanced carotenoids extraction yield of 65,350 μg/g from orange peels was achieved using hydrophobic deep eutectic solvents (DES) consisting of Menthol and Camphor, along with an ultrasound extraction method with an ultrasound intensity of 60 % and an extraction time of 20 min (Vi ̃nas-Ospino et al., 2023). Pomegranate juice processing plants produce a significant amount of waste, comprising fruit residues such as seeds and outer peels. The peel, consisting of the exocarp and mesocarp, accounts for approximately 50 % of the total weight of the fresh pomegranate fruit, and its chemical composition varies depending on cultivar type (Lacivita et al., 2023). This amounts to over 1.9 million metric tons per year globally (El Barnossi et al., 2021). The exceptional properties of pomegranate fruit peels (Punica granatum L.) are well-documented, as they contain a plethora of bioactive phytoconstituents, notably phenolic compounds like hydrolyzable ellagitannins, anthocyanins, and flavonoids (Singh et al., 2019). These compounds are renowned for their antioxidant, antimicrobial,
antifungal, antiviral, and antidiabetic activities (George et al., 2019; Lacivita et al., 2023). Waste and by-products generated from tomatoes, the second most important vegetable crop Worldwide, are rich sources of carotenoids (lycopene, beta-carotene, lutein), flavonoids, phenolic acids, tannins, dietary fibers, and vitamin C (Khatib et al., 2023). These compounds contribute to the potential health benefits of tomato by-products, including antioxidant and anti-inflammatory effects, cardiovascular protection, and potential anticancer properties (Natali et al., 2023). Recently, Khatib et al. (2023) reported a polysaccharide yield of 6 % (w/ w based on dry matter) for dried tomato by-products using water at 100 ◦C. They revealed that tomato polysaccharides are rich in rhamnose, with galacturonic acid content ranging from 22.3 % to 33.8 %. Additionally, they found that their degree of methylation (DM) and degree of acetylation (DA) varied from 8.86 % to 71.00 % and from 8.57 % to 19.42 %, respectively, depending on the solvent nature and temperature (Khatib et al., 2023). The observed variation in DA and DM levels could influence the functional and biological characteristics of polysaccharides recovered from plant by-products and waste, thereby determining their suitability for use in food and cosmetic industries. Polysaccharides extracted from the by-products of red bell peppers, a versatile vegetable cultivated and utilized globally, demonstrate notable anti-inflammatory, anti-complement, immunomodulatory, and chemopreventive properties (Adami et al., 2018). Additionally, these polysaccharides can be extracted at a yield nearing 10 % (Khatib et al., 2023). Red bell peppers also contain significant quantities of vitamin C, dietary fibers, flavonoids such as quercetin and luteolin, and carotenoids (Anaya-Esparza et al., 2021). Dietary fibers, which form the structural framework of plant cell walls, comprise both soluble fibers, such as mucilage and pectin, and insoluble fibers, like lignin and cellulose. In lemon, orange, and grapefruit seeds, the content of soluble dietary fibers varied between 4.6 % and 7.9 %, while insoluble dietary fibers ranged from 75.9 % to 82.2 % (Karaman et al., 2017). The total dietary fibers in banana and mango peels were 64.3 % and 69.8 %, respectively (Tlais et al., 2020). Banana peels are mainly rich in insoluble fibers (56.8 %) like cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin, but contains also substantial amounts of pectin (Arun et al., 2015). Dietary fiber consumption has been linked to numerous health advantages, such as a decreased risk of diabetes, cancer, hypertension, obesity, and cardiovascular and gastrointestinal disorders (Hasan et al., 2024; Tlais et al., 2020).
