Carrots, commonly juiced for their refreshing taste, result in a substantial number of by-products – up to 40 % of the original weight (Tlais et al., 2020). These by-products, mainly carrot pomace and peels, contain a high concentration of valuable compounds like dietary fibers (pectin), carotenoids (β-carotene), phenolic acids, minerals and vitamins. Thanks to their antioxidant, antimicrobial, anti-inflammatory and antimutagenic activities (Gulsunoglu et al., 2019), carrot by-products are gaining traction as a promising source of natural ingredients for functional foods or additives. Date palm by-products are made of fibrous materials mainly generated during harvest (leaflets, mesh, rachis, spadix stems, and petiole) and date processing by-products (immature or spoiled dates, pollen, seeds, and press cake). Date palm biomass contains many valuable compounds, including dietary fibers, flavanols, carotenoids, phytosterols, and tocopherols (Manai et al., 2024). Many innovative extraction methods have proven to be effective in extracting phenolics, dietary fibers, flavonoids, carotenoids, and carbohydrates from date palm biomass, including ultrasonication, subcritical water extraction, subcritical fluid CO2 extraction, subcritical fluid extraction, and microwave-assisted extraction-deep eutectic solvent extraction (Ghafoor et al., 2022; Kehili et al., 2024). Large amounts of by-products are produced during the extraction of olive oil, including leave, pomace stones, and wastewater. Olive byproducts are cheap and constitute a source of bioactive and functional compounds with a wide range of health benefits (Otero et al., 2021). These bioactive compounds include for leaves (phenolic compounds, mainly secoiridoids, flavonoids and substituted phenols); pomace (phenolic compounds, essential amino acids, sugars, organic acids, and mineral nutrients); stones (cellulose, hemicelluloses, lignin, and phenolic compounds), and wastewater (phenolic compounds, pectic
polysaccharides, and lignocellulosic residues) (Khwaldia et al., 2022).
2.2. Grain/cereal by-products
Global cereal production, surpassing 2.8 billion tons annually, generates substantial by-products (FAO, 2023). Around 400 million tons of corn, wheat, and rice harvest residues are burned yearly (Salazar-L ́opez et al., 2019). Industrial processing steps (milling, decortication, etc.) discard by-products like the pericarp, aleurone layer, and germ. Cereal by-products represent a valuable source of dietary fibers (e.g., β-glucans), proteins, bioactive peptides, lipids, phenolic compounds, phytosterols, and other health-promoting elements (Fa ̆rcaș et al., 2022). Brewer's spent grain (BSG), the main by-product of beer production, represents a significant waste stream. With global beer output reaching around 180 million tons annually, BSG production is estimated at roughly 20 % of that volume (L ́opez-Linares et al., 2020). This complex mixture, primarily composed of barley husks and seed coat fragments, boasts a valuable nutritional profile. BSG contains 30-50 % carbohydrates and 20-30 % protein, making it a potential source of valuable bioproducts (Karlsen and Skov, 2022). However, its perishable nature and complex composition have traditionally limited its use to low-value animal feed. While research explores utilizing BSG to extract valuable compounds like arabinoxylan and prebiotic oligosaccharides (Sun et al., 2024), achieving economic viability for large-scale production remains a challenge. A recent study by Sun et al. (2024) proposed a promising and cost-effective approach for industrial BSG valorization. This three-step method utilizes ammoniation pretreatment, enzymatic hydrolysis, and fermentation to convert BSG into valuable products: single-cell protein (SCP) and arabinoxylo-oligosaccharides (prebiotics). Notably, the process successfully yielded from every 1 kg of BSG approximately 310 g of
