SCP and 80 g of prebiotics per kg of BSG. Several cereal processing by-products from large-scale industries, such as BSG, rice bran, wheat bran, and corn bran, have been identified for their high protein content, making them suitable candidates for producing hydrolysates with promising biological effects (Tlais et al., 2020). Extensive research findings emphasize the feasibility of deriving bioactive peptides from proteins sourced from brewer's spent grain and rice bran, underscoring the significant value of these extracted proteins from industrial by-products. These bioactive peptides, composed of 2–50 amino acids, exert beneficial effects on human health and food products, including lowering blood pressure, scavenging free radicals, and exhibiting antimicrobial properties (Gobbetti et al., 2018). Cereal waste and by-products serve as rich sources of essential vitamins and micronutrients, primarily concentrated in the bran and cereal germs. These processing discards are rich in both macrominerals (calcium, magnesium, etc.) and essential trace minerals (zinc, iron, etc.) (Z. Jin et al., 2022). Additionally, cereal by-products contain significant quantities of B vitamins, including niacin, pantothenic acid, and thiamine (Verni et al., 2019). Notably, BSG primarily contains choline at
1800 mg⋅kg 1, followed by smaller amounts of niacin, pantothenic acid, riboflavin, and thiamine. Recovering valuable bioactive compounds from BSG requires careful selection of processing methods to maximize their utilization (Lech and Labus, 2022). Common techniques focus on converting BSG's cell wall components (cellulose and hemicellulose) into fermentable sugars through chemical and enzymatic hydrolysis. This process liberates functional compounds trapped within the BSG structure. Similarly, strategic extraction methods and pre-treatments can significantly enhance the yield of vitamins and minerals from cereal by-products (Lech and Labus, 2022). Rice bran boasts the highest reported lipid content among cereal brans at nearly 19 % dry weight, followed by wheat germ and corn bran (G ́ornas ́ et al., 2016). These lipids are rich in health-promoting unsaturated fatty acids, particularly oleic and linoleic acids (Xu et al., 2021). Additionally, rice bran contains a variety of bioactive compounds like tocopherols, squalene, and phytosterols, which contribute to its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties (Xu et al., 2021). A byproducts of wheat milling, wheat germ is a significant source of valuable nutrients, generating an estimated 25 million tons globally each year (Fa ̆rcaș et al., 2022). It boasts a wealth of health-promoting compounds, including biopeptides, polyunsaturated fatty acids, and functional components like sterols, tocopherols, and antioxidants (Tlais et al., 2020). On the other hand, wheat bran, a by-products of wheat milling (around 15 % of the grain's weight) is rich in fiber, protein, and healthy fats. Wheat bran is known to aid digestion, reduce the risk of chronic diseases, and potentially have anti-cancer effects (Deroover et al., 2020). Wheat bran's valuable composition includes dietary fibers, polyphenols, sterols, and β-glucans, contributing to its health properties (Fa ̆rcaș et al., 2022). Corn bran is rich in phenolic acids, with ferulic acid being the most abundant, followed by vanillic, caffeic, and other acids (Fa ̆rcaș et al., 2022). Additionally, corn bran is rich in anthocyanins, making them valuable food pigments (Tlais et al., 2020).
2.3. Oilseed press cakes
Beyond their primary role in oil production, oilseed processing generates valuable by-products known as oilseed meals. These meals can boast a protein content ranging from 15 to 50 % (Vinayashree and Vasu, 2021) and hold significant potential as protein sources for human nutrition. Furthermore, oilseed by-products offer economic value due to their content in bioactive compounds. Pumpkin seeds, for example, contain significant levels of carotenoids, phenolics, tocopherols, and phytosterols (Rabrenovi ́c et al., 2014). Flaxseeds are rich in essential fatty acids and lignans, while chia and sesame seeds boast polyunsaturated fatty acids, tocopherols, and lignans (Gahfoor et al., 2018). Grapeseeds, also contain essential fatty acids, tocopherols, and catechins
(Al Juhaimi and  ̈Ozcan, 2018). The protein content of these oilseed by
products can reach up to 40 %, with in vitro protein digestibility ranging from 70 to 85 %. Analysis of anti-nutritional factors revealed low phytic acid content across all studied seed meals, but high tannin content was observed specifically in grapeseed meal (Sa ́ et al., 2021). Extracted via cold pressing, chia seed oil stands out as a nutritious botanical source rich in omega-3 fatty acids. This oil boasts not only high levels of these essential fats but also a significant protein and fiber content (Ferreira et al., 2023). Moreover, the cold-pressing process yields a cost-effective by-product, rich in polysaccharides, which further enhances the overall resource potential of chia seeds. Notably, a natural gelling agent, a gum, can be extracted from this by-product as a substitute for gelatin and whey protein in bigel production (Alkabaa et al., 2024). Defatted sunflower flour, a by-product of extracting oil from sunflower seeds, is traditionally utilized in animal feed. Nonetheless, it offers a plentiful and economical protein source suitable for human consumption. Sunflower seeds are rich in storage proteins, with 11S globulins being the most abundant (accounting for 60-80 % of total protein), followed by 2S albumins (20-30 %). Tonolo et al. (2024) investigated bioactive peptides derived from sunflower protein isolate (SPI), obtained from defatted sunflower flour. Following simulated gastrointestinal digestion, the resulting peptide-enriched fraction exhibited antioxidant properties in zebrafish, a live animal model (Tonolo et al., 2024). Though the exact composition can vary depending on the variety, hempseeds boast an impressive nutritional profile. They pack a powerful punch with 30-35 % of their weight in healthy fats (lipids), 20-25 % protein, 30-35 % dietary fiber, and a wealth of essential minerals (Lan et al., 2019). Rich in essential fatty acids, primarily linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid, as well as α- and γ-tocopherols, hempseeds are prized for their high oil content and unique fatty acid profile. Interestingly, the cake obtained from hempseed oil extraction serves as a valuable source of proteins, demonstrating favorable technofunctional properties such as a high solubility index, emulsifying ability, and stability (MendozaP ́erez et al., 2024). Due to their protein content, olive seeds, separated from the olive flesh during the pitting step, have been used for extracting protein hydrolysates rich in bioactive peptides through enzymatic processes. These hydrolysates have demonstrated antioxidant, antidiabetic and cholesterol-lowering properties. Moreover, they showed stability during simulated intestinal absorption using human intestinal cell models (Bartolomei et al., 2024). Another by-product of olive oil production, olive pomace, is the solid residue derived from the process. It retains a notable quantity of residual oil and offers a source of triterpenes. These include maslinic and oleanolic acids (triterpenic acids) as well as erythrodiol and uvaol (triterpene dialcohols) (Schmidt et al., 2023). Prickly pear seeds, comprising 2-3.8 % of the fruit, yield oil rich in unsaturated fatty acids, primarily linoleic acid (Al-Naqeb et al., 2021). The oil also boasts significant levels of vitamins (tocopherols, vitamin K1) and sterols (β-sitosterol being dominant). Notably, γ-tocopherol is the major tocopherol, outnumbering α and δ forms (Al-Naqeb et al., 2021). However, the oil's composition, especially fatty acids and tocopherols, varies depending on geographic origin (Ettalibi et al., 2021). Prickly pear seed oil exhibits antioxidant and antimicrobial properties, antidiabetic potential, lipid-lowering effects, in vitro anticancer activity, anti-inflammatory properties, anti-ulcer effects, and protection against UV radiation (Al-Naqeb et al., 2021; Ferreira et al., 2024). Prickly pear seed pomace, a by-product of seed oil extraction, is abundant in phenolic compounds and exhibits potent antioxidant properties, along with modest anti-inflammatory and anti-tyrosinase activities. Phenolic extracts obtained through an autohydrolysis process hold the potential for use in cosmetic formulations due to their effective UV-screening abilities (Ferreira et al., 2024). Cold-pressed Sacha Inchi seed oil, extracted from the seeds of the oleaginous Sacha Inchi plant, is prized for its richness in essential fatty acids like α-linolenic acid and tocopherols. This oil has gained
