3. Emerging techniques for recovering bioactive compounds
Emerging extraction methods aligned with the “Green Extraction Process” concept have been developed, prioritizing renewable resources, alternative solvents, energy efficiency, waste minimization, process safety, and high-quality extract production (Chemat et al., 2012). The use of green extraction methods such as microwave assisted extraction (MAE), ultrasound-assisted extraction (UAE), enzymeassisted extraction (EAE), supercritical fluid extraction (SFE), pressurized liquid extraction (PLE), pulsed electric field (PEF), and membrane separation technology (MST), to convert plant-based waste and byproducts into valuable bioactive compounds can promote a more sustainable model by reducing waste and generating new revenue streams. The MAE technique utilizes non-ionizing microwave radiation (frequency 0.3–300 GHz) to rapidly heat solvents through interaction with polar molecules within plant matrices, facilitating analyte extraction (Bitwell et al., 2023). Essential oils from citrus peels, phenolic compounds from apple pomace, grape skins, olive mill wastewater, cereal brans, soybean meal, rapeseed meal, and sunflower seed meal, pigments from tomato and carrot peels, and blueberry pomace, and polysaccharides from apple pomace, citrus peels, sugar beet pulp, wheat and oat brans, corncobs, and rice husk are examples of successfully extracted bioactive compounds by MAE (Alvi et al., 2022). MAE offers significant advantages, including reduced processing time, solvent consumption, and energy usage, while producing higher-quality extracts at lower costs. UAE employs high-frequency sound waves to disrupt plant matrices and enhance the extraction of target compounds. Cavitation, induced by the rapid formation and collapse of bubbles, generates localized high temperatures and pressures, leading to cell disruption and increased mass transfer (Kumar et al., 2021). UAE is an efficient technique with reduced extraction time and energy consumption, particularly suited for extracting thermolabile compounds, though it may induce degradation due to the formation of free radicals (Hasan et al., 2024). UAE has been used to extract polysaccharides from wheat bran, rice husk, apple pomace, pomegranate and grape skins; anthocyanins from blueberry and raspberry pomace, cherry pomace, and blackcurrant pomace; and carotenoids from tomato peels, papaya peel and pulp, mango pomace, pomegranate waste, and carrot pomace (Martins et al., 2023). EAE utilizes enzymes to disrupt plant cell structures, enhancing the release of phytochemicals. This green and efficient method reduces the use of solvents and energy compared to traditional extraction techniques. EAE has been employed to improve the extraction of a wide range of compounds, including phenolic compounds such as flavonoids and anthocyanidins from saffron tepals, wheat bran, pistachio green hulls, bean shells, and brewers' spent grain; carotenoids from tomato waste; and biopolymers from grapefruit peel, buckwheat husk, palm kernel, and soybean meal (Streimikyte et al., 2022). However, challenges include high costs, scalability issues, and enzyme limitations in breaking down plant cell walls. SFE is a sustainable and eco-friendly method for extracting valuable compounds from plant-based waste and by-products. This process utilizes solvents, primarily carbon dioxide (CO2), under specific temperature and pressure conditions that transform the CO2 into a supercritical fluid. This state allows for efficient solubilization of lipophilic compounds (e.g., carotenoids, essential oils, and flavonoids) due to the fluid's unique properties, including low density, viscosity, and surface tension, combined with high diffusivity (Gan and Baroutian, 2022).
Despite its efficacy in extracting heat-sensitive compounds, SFE requires precise control of operating parameters (e.g. solvent flow, pressure and temperature) and faces challenges related to cost and continuous operation. PLE employs solvent extraction at high temperatures and pressures to enhance analyte recovery while maintaining the solvent in a liquid state throughout the process. This technique disrupts cellular structures, liberating both polar and non-polar compounds, including essential oils from coriander seeds and citrus peels, carotenoids from tomato pomace and carrot roots, anthocyanins from grape skins and blackberry fruits, phenolics from oregano by-products and olive leaves, proteins from soybean meal, and carbohydrates from potato peels (Barp et al., 2023). PLE is a rapid, efficient, and solvent-saving technique that enhances analyte extraction through increased temperature and pressure. However, the high cost of specialized equipment limits its widespread application. By applying short, high-voltage pulses, PEF induces electroporation, creating pores in cell walls and enhancing mass transfer. This nonthermal, green, and cost-effective process has been effectively used to release polyphenols, flavonoids, and anthocyanins from plum stems and pomace, white mulberry leaves, olive leaves, blueberry press cake, apricot pomace and kernels, quince peels, chicory roots, cocoa bean shells, custard apple leaves, and flaxseed hulls; steroidal alkaloids from potato peels; polysaccharides from jackfruit waste and corn silk; and proteins from rapeseed stems and leaves (Chatzimitakos et al., 2023). Membrane filtration, including nanofiltration, microfiltration, and ultrafiltration, offers a sustainable and solvent-free approach for isolating phytochemicals from plant-based waste and by-products. MST has gained significant attention in the industry for its ability to concentrate, purify, and separate valuable compounds. Offering high recovery rates and selectivity, MST is particularly effective for recovering phenolics (e.g., phenolic acids, catechol, tyrosol, and hydroxytyrosol) from wine lees, grape marc, fruit seeds, and corn, olive, and artichoke wastewaters (Castro-Mun ̃oz et al., 2020). Industry 4.0 technologies offer significant opportunities to enhance the sustainability of green and novel extraction processes. By integrating digital tools for energy efficiency, waste reduction, process optimization, and traceability, these processes can contribute to a more circular and environmentally friendly manufacturing landscape. Real-time monitoring, predictive maintenance, renewable energy integration, data-driven decision-making, automation, and blockchain technology are key elements in achieving these goals.
4. Plant-based waste and by-products and food sustainability
Food sustainability and more broadly sustainable development requires an integrated approach that takes into consideration environmental and social concerns along with economic development. Every year, a staggering 1.3 billion tonnes of food are wasted or lost worldwide (Dura ́n-Sandoval et al., 2023). According to Roy et al. (2023) developed countries, with a total population of 1.4 billion, threw away around 670 million tonnes of edible food, while developing countries, with a population of 6.2 billion, contributed around 630 million tonnes. These figures stand in stark contrast to the global reality, where between 690 and 828 million people suffer from hunger, and 3 billion cannot afford a healthy diet (FAO et al., 2022; Roy et al., 2023).
4.1. Environmental and social dimension
Reducing food waste not only benefits the environment but also contributes to various social agendas by reducing greenhouse gas emissions, enhancing ecosystem services, and enforcing sustainable resource management policies (Table 1). Food waste accounts for 8-10 % of global greenhouse gas emissions and ranks as the third-largest emitter globally, following China and the United States (S. You et al., 2022).
