Utilizing industrial food waste for higher value-added purposes could save significant CO2 equivalent emissions (RedCorn et al., 2018; Roy et al., 2023). However, caution is advised regarding energy recovery from food waste incineration due to its high moisture content (Kim and Song, 2014). Anaerobic digestion is identified as a preferable option over incineration, with decentralized anaerobic digestion offering the highest environmental benefit. Recycling and reusing agricultural waste and animal dung for biogas production are promising avenues for sustainable waste management and energy production (Koul et al., 2022). The environmental impact of food waste management varies depending on the method employed, with landfilling having the highest impact and animal feeding the least. Discrepancies among studies are attributed to methodological choices and geographical variations (Roy et al., 2023).
4.2. Economic dimension
Selecting the right approach for valorizing food waste is crucial for cost-effective management and resource conservation (Roy et al., 2023). The economic impact of food waste is closely tied to the valorization processes used, with economic benefits depending on the derived endproducts (Table 1) (Kotykova and Babych, 2019). The significant economic cost of lost and wasted food, estimated at U.S. $680 billion in developed countries and U.S. $310 billion in developing countries, underscores the urgency of effective management strategies (Laso et al., 2020). While some studies question the economic viability of food waste anaerobic digestion, emphasizing the need for additional revenue streams such as using biogas for vehicles or on-grid electricity to enhance economic benefits (Flotats, 2019) others assert its feasibility, particularly when supported by government subsidies (Papirio et al., 2020; Roy et al., 2023). In the United States, converting industrial food waste into lactic acids or biogas is estimated to generate significant annual revenue (Papirio et al., 2020). Incineration is identified as having the least life cycle cost compared to other waste management options, though decentralized composting of food waste is economically beneficial, with examples like on-site incineration at Hong Kong International Airport showing promising sustainability and economic savings (Lam et al., 2018). The economic viability of food waste biorefineries hinges on factors like plant capacity, product diversity, and integration with existing infrastructure (Roy et al., 2023). Studies have examined various economic indicators to assess the profitability of food waste recovery scenarios, highlighting the influence of market prices and economies of scale (Crist ́obal et al., 2018). Integrating food waste biorefineries into existing facilities can significantly reduce production costs and enhance economic sustainability (Lohrasbi et al., 2010). Overall, a comprehensive economic evaluation of food waste valorization requires considering waste stream availability and proximity to biorefinery locations, factors often overlooked in previous studies (Caldeira et al., 2020). Such considerations are essential for ensuring the economic sustainability of food waste management practices.
