rganic waste into compost. By utilizing data on environmental conditions, waste characteristics, and microbial activities, their model accurately estimated composting yield and identified optimal conditions to maximize biogas production. AI can be utilized to optimize waste valorization processes such as composting or methanization. For instance, predictive models based on AI can be developed to monitor and control composting conditions to optimize the organic waste decomposition process and produce highquality compost. A study conducted by Chiu et al. (2022) employed
ML techniques (based on random forest and long short-term memory) to predict biogas production from food waste based on factors such as waste composition, temperature, and humidity. These predictions enable the adjustment of methanization process parameters to maximize energy yield. Another significant application is presented by You et al. (2017), who compared several ML models for online classification of the calorific value of municipal solid waste burned in circulating fluidized bed incinerators. By utilizing techniques such as artificial neural networks (ANN), support vector machines (SVM), and random forests (RF), they developed an online classification system capable of rapidly identifying the composition and energy value of burned waste, thereby optimizing the combustion process and energy production. Furthermore, Neto et al. (2021) used ANN for modelling production biogas from fruit and vegetable waste and predicting biogas yield and composition. The response surfaces of the ANN model were found to be useful for defining the optimum region in biogas production. It was observed that biodigestion of fruit and vegetable waste could lead to a highest cumulative biogas production. In summary, AI provides innovative solutions for optimizing waste valorization processes, particularly through material recognition, classification, and biogas production forecasting. These advancements contribute to better management of organic resources and the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions, aligning industrial practices with the goals of a circular and sustainable economy.
