application of IoT lies in controlling and measuring bioprocess parameters. Indeed, precise control of the numerous components influencing bioprocesses is crucial to meet the growing demands of research and production. These components include the concentration of nutrients and metabolites, cell density, pH, oxygen level, humidity, and temperature (Neeba et al., 2021). Real-time measurement of these parameters is complex but essential. Spectroscopic methods such as UV–visible, mid-infrared, near-infrared, fluorescence, and Raman show potential for real-time monitoring of various parameters but suffer from limitations such as poor discernment and data processing complexity (Rusli et al., 2021). The IoT emerges as a promising solution, enabling real-time monitoring of key parameters such as humidity, temperature, pH, and oxygen level, thanks to innovative sensors and microfabrication techniques (Chau et al., 2021; Hassoun et al., 2022c; Van et al., 2022). Recent studies have shown that IoT facilitates the monitoring and management of bioprocesses, particularly in food waste valorization (Kalsoom et al., 2021; Kazemi et al., 2020; Nath and Singh, 2021). However, challenges remain, such as the complexity of sensor and data network architecture, as well as potential security and compatibility issues (Melikoglu, 2020). Only a few studies have described the application of IoT for controlling plant-based waste in food processing process. For instance, Jagtap et al. (2019) developed an automated, realtime system based IoT to monitor the global quantity of waste and the reasons for its production in real time throughout the potato transformation and processing industry, employing advanced image processing and sensor technology. The authors utilized the images captured and processed by a specially positioned camera to recognize damaged and unusable products, while a digital scale was applied to measure their weight. Using IoT resulted in cost savings and reduced the risk of unwanted potatoes finding their way into the final food product (Jagtap et al., 2019). In the same context, at a different level of the food production and distribution process, Gayathri et al. (2021) and Luthra et al. (2018) have also explored the use of IoT to mitigate food waste. Gayathri et al. (2021) employed RFID sensors to manage individual food waste according to a suggested model, while Luthra et al. (2018) outlined the implementation of IoT-based technologies in supply chain management within developing nations. These studies highlight the diverse applications of IoT in addressing food waste challenges across various stages of the food production and distribution process. In conclusion, the IoT plays a crucial role in real-time monitoring and control of bioprocess parameters, which is essential for enhancing the valorization of plant-based food waste. IoT solutions not only optimize industrial processes but also reduce costs and risks, thereby strengthening the efficiency and sustainability of the food industry.
5.3. Additive manufacturing or 3D Printing
With its potential for a wide range of uses in the food business, additive manufacturing, often known as 3D printing, is an emerging technology that presents a fresh approach to food processing and the creation of novel formulas (Lv et al., 2023; Piyush et al., 2020). The method is designed to swiftly and simply create aesthetically pleasing and nutrient-dense food items with little to no human involvement and without costly tooling, resulting in more effective resource usage and little to no waste (Lee et al., 2020). A great deal of work is being done on 3D printing technologies and given the immense promise and potential applications of this technology, it is even becoming a new industrial revolution (Campbell et al., 2011). Through the use of 3D printing technology, plant waste and byproducts can be transformed in new, value-added products (Godoi et al., 2016). This approach provides the food industry with the opportunity to create innovative products tailored to consumer preferences and market demand (Lipton et al., 2015). For example, Grape pomace was collected from industries and utilized as a key ingredient of a 3D printing material for functional cookies (Jagadiswaran et al., 2021).
They noted an increase in viscosity as a function of grape content, leading to the use of a lower print speed and extrusion rate to improve printability. Similar observations were reported by Mildner-Szkudlarz et al. (2013), who optimized the printing process of dough consisting of cracked wheat flour and various concentrations of grape pomace powder. Functional cookies exhibited improved palatability, shape stability, higher levels of antioxidant properties and less anthocyanin content loss and increased fiber and protein contents. In another study, Leo et al. (2022) designed 3D-printed snacks enriched in vitamins and nutrients by using waste orange peel. The developed snacks presented a high content in bioflavonoids and antioxidant activity. Molina-Montero et al. (2023) by using orange peel ink found that orange by-products had a significant effect on the print process and final snack quality. All the developed formulations showed a more pronounced elastic behavior and greater crunch. Muthurajan et al. (2021) designed ready-to-cook noodles from waste potato peelings, comparing the texture, cooking quality, and consumer acceptance with commercial noodles. The resulted product proved to be rich in nutrients and suitable for various forms or layering. Moreover, potato waste has been used by Feng et al. (2020) to develop a yam-based snack using 3D printing. Additionally, the study of Derossi et al. (2018) deployed 3D printing techniques in order to create a fruit-based snack for children. Regarding ink formulation, research conducted by Pant et al. (2023) investigated recipes for food inks formulated from green leafy vegetables such as spinach and kale stems. They characterized these wastes on their rheological properties to develop soft, easy-to-chew 3D-printed foods for patients suffering from dysphagia (difficulty swallowing). Furthermore, other researchers investigated the valorization of banana peel and sugarcane bagasse into food packaging using 3D printing (Nida et al., 2022). The paper explored the impact of combining banana peel and sugarcane bagasse in different ratios (1:1, 1:9, 9:1) along with the incorporation of guar gum for 3D food packaging personalization. Jackfruit seeds are known to be a rich source of starch, protein, fiber, and magnesium, known for promoting good bone health and lowering blood pressure. The study of Wong et al. (2022) indicates the feasibility of 3D printing jackfruit seeds after pre-treatment with a food-grade ink suitable for 3D printing. The researchers concluded that food waste like jackfruit seeds can be designed and packaged into readily consumable snacks and meals: biscuits, cookies, wafers, and chips. 3D printing is a useful technique for transforming unconventional food components such as jackfruit seeds through aesthetic and visually appetizing shapes, thus promoting consumer acceptance. 3D printing offers promising prospects for transforming plant waste into high-value products, such as functional snacks and customized food packaging. This innovative technology not only reduces waste but also meets the growing market demand for more sustainable and personalized products.
5.4. Smart sensors
Kerry (2012) identified sensors as a device utilized to detect, locate and/or quantify energy or matter, providing a signal either for the identification or measurement of a physical or chemical property. Cloud computing services provide possibilities for data processing, storage, and collecting that let sensors reach their full potential. The integration of smart sensors with IoT, AI, and other Industry 4.0 technologies has made smart sensors a crucial component of contemporary agriculture and food supply chains, offering valuable data for executive decisionmaking. Smart sensors as part of a wide range of production equipment are used more and more in the food industry to monitor, control and optimize numerous manufacturing processes in real time, while at the same time improving food traceability and quality (Watson et al., 2021). As food products evolve and production and processing conditions change, smart sensors are designed to be tiny, flexible, and versatile.
