5.5. Digital twins
Digital twins involve creating digital representations of real-world products, processes, or physical objects. These representations integrate various technological advancements like IoT and AI to synchronize physical activities with the virtual world. Digital twins come in statistical, data-driven, and physics-based models (Defraeye et al., 2021; Tao et al., 2019; Van Der Burg et al., 2021; Verboven et al., 2020). They hold the potential to enhance knowledge and streamline decision-making across different sectors such as agriculture and food processing (Defraeye et al., 2021; Van Der Burg et al., 2021; Verboven et al., 2020). Furthermore, they could aid in predicting postharvest changes in food quality and optimizing supply chains to minimize food loss (Defraeye et al., 2021; Onwude et al., 2024). Only a few studies have described the application of digital twins in Transforming plant-based waste into valuable products. For instance, Kümmritz et al. (2024) investigated the potential of digital twins to enhance sustainability in the cultivation and utilization of medicinal and aromatic plants. Their research highlights how these precise virtual models can not only optimize agricultural practices for these plants but also open new avenues in the extraction methods of active compounds. Digital twins provide detailed simulations and analyses of plant growth conditions, enabling farmers to optimize resource use (such as water and nutrients) reduce waste, and increase yields. Additionally, these virtual models offer innovative solutions for more efficient and environmentally friendly extraction processes of beneficial plant compounds, such as essential oils and medicinal substances. Moreover, digital twins present a valuable alternative for recycling organic waste generated by these plants by converting it into biofertilizers. This approach not only minimizes waste but also enriches the soil, thereby closing the production cycle in a sustainable manner. Overall, digital twins hold significant promise for making the cultivation and use of medicinal and aromatic plants more sustainable and efficient while providing novel solutions for organic waste management. Finally, digital twins represent an emerging technology with considerable potential to optimize the cultivation and valorization of medicinal and aromatic plants. By simulating growth conditions and improving extraction processes, these virtual models not only reduce waste but also enrich the soil, fostering more sustainable agriculture and
a more environmentally friendly production cycle.
5.6. Robotics
In recent years, major food firms have increasingly integrated robotics into their food manufacturing plants, significantly contributing to the reduction and valorization of food loss and waste, particularly of plant origin (Duong et al., 2020). In the agricultural and food industries, robots now perform a wide range of tasks, especially when combined with intelligent sensors, such as advanced cameras and vision systems, and AI. These technologies enable precise, real-time monitoring and decision-making, enhancing efficiency and reducing waste. Robots may be broadly categorized into five types: collaborative robots, articulated robots, delta robots, cartesian robots, and selective compliance assembly robot arm (SCARA) robots (Riboli et al., 2023). These autonomous systems offer several key advantages in food processing. Robots can handle delicate and complex tasks quickly and skillfully, minimizing damage to plant-based products during processing and packaging. By maintaining high levels of cleanliness and precision, robots help reduce the risk of contamination, thereby preserving food safety and extending shelf life, which directly cuts down on food loss. Robots can operate efficiently in various environments and perform tasks under specific conditions that might be challenging for human workers, further preventing potential waste. (Aly et al., 2023). Robotics plays a crucial role in transforming plant-based waste and by-products into valuable products. Through precise sorting, separation, and processing, robots can identify and repurpose usable materials that might otherwise be discarded. This not only reduces waste but also creates new revenue streams by converting by-products into ingredients for other food products, animal feed, or bio-based materials, thus contributing to a more sustainable and circular food system (I ̃niguezMoreno et al., 2023; Kshirsagar et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2022; Olawade et al., 2024; Sarc et al., 2019). For example, Onoda (2020) indicated the potential of robotic sorting systems powered by artificial intelligence algorithms to separate and sort waste, streamline the sorting process, and increase recycling rates. In this process, a stereo camera recognizes images and distances, and the robotic arm automatically sorts them (Ahmed and Asadullah, 2020). Moreover, Soheyb et al. (2021) have proposed a solution based on robotics and IoT technologies for the management of smart greenhouses that offers the farmer the possibility of monitoring, supervising and controlling a large number of greenhouses to minimize waste and damaged production without needing to physically intervene each time for regular actions such as irrigation, fogging and aeration according to the needs of food and plants.
5.7. Blockchain
Blockchain technology serves as a real-time, decentralized digital ledger that securely records and manages transactions across a distributed network. Each transaction, or “block,” is linked in a chain that is immutable and transparent, ensuring data integrity and accessibility (Antonucci et al., 2019; Prashar et al., 2020). This technology is particularly valuable in the food supply chain, where it enables the precise tracking and monitoring of food products from farm to table. By utilizing blockchain, data related to the production, transportation, and processing of food can be securely recorded and easily accessed, reducing inefficiencies and mitigating food loss and waste, especially in plant-based products (Carey and Subramanian, 2021; K ̈or et al., 2022; Wünsche and Fernqvist, 2022). The ability of blockchain to provide detailed, tamper-proof records ensures that every step in the supply chain is monitored, which helps in identifying and addressing points where food loss occurs. Moreover, the technology facilitates the valorization of food waste by enabling the tracking and management of plant-based by-products, transforming them into valuable secondary products. By ensuring that these by
